  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 225 PM EDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nA surface ridge of high pressure and dry northwest flow over the \nsouthern Appalachians is pulling drier air into the region. Due to \nclear sky and calm winds, steam fog off area lakes and streams are \nexpected so patchy areas of fog is expected near the waterways. some \nof the fog will be locally dense.\n\nThe surface ridge will move east of the area for Tuesday allowing \nmoisture to pull back into the Tennessee valley along with a weak \ninverted trough. Instability remains quite limited with PWs of only \n0.7 to 0.9 so convection is not anticipated. \n\nDry northwest flow aloft will remain through Wednesday with a dry \nfrontal boundary moving the area late Thursday. Reinforcing dry air \nis expected for Friday with surface ridging across the southern \nAppalachians. PWs for Friday will drop to 0.6 at TYS. \n\nA lot question marks for this weekend. Tropical system has a 70 \npercent chance of developing into a depression.  Where this system \neventually turns north or northeastward is the uncertainty. Some of \nthe deterministic and ensemble models pull moisture back into the \nTennessee and southern Appalachians and early next week.\n\nThere is a great deal of uncertainty in the development and path of \nthe expected tropical depression over the Gulf of America.\nDepending on the exact path, heavy rainfall and gusty winds are \npossible this weekend.",
